, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7412fb89-6dd8-5b4d-80b7-8e6b94b89a77', 'word_count': 2374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7412fb89-6dd8-5b4d-80b7-8e6b94b89a77'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a dad’s plea for members of the faith to demand the resignation of the entire clergy, including the pope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/01/opinion/catholic-church-sexual-abuse-scandal-reform.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a dad’s plea for members of the faith to demand the resignation of the entire clergy, including the pope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weekends ago, Naka Nathaniel stood up during a Mass in Atlanta and confronted his priest about the Roman Catholic Church’s response to the Pennsylvania sexual abuse cover-up. In an Op-Ed essay last week, Mr. Nathaniel wrote about balancing his already complex relationship with Catholicism with his role as a father raising a 9-year-old son in the Catholic faith. He concluded that the church can no longer be reformed from within. “I’m mad at the church administration,” he wrote. But, he added, “I’m also angry at the congregation. I’m upset with the people who aren’t demanding that every member of the clergy resign.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/23/opinion/23nathaniel/23nathaniel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Catholic Church Beyond Redemption?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-01T18:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Tarchak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tarchak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d7db4f-639c-5fd7-a515-00d9dc80033c', 'word_count': 1580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d7db4f-639c-5fd7-a515-00d9dc80033c'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans have weaponized the judicial nomination process as a purely partisan exercise. Democrats, given the chance, will likely return the favor. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes