ndy Shmerler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cindy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shmerler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55480695-8499-5df8-a13a-8f28d81f6787', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55480695-8499-5df8-a13a-8f28d81f6787'}, {'abstract': 'The Eastern monarch is in trouble, and this is the time to help (no science degree needed).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/03/opinion/to-save-monarch-butterfly-plant-milkweed-now.html', 'snippet': 'The Eastern monarch is in trouble, and this is the time to help (no science degree needed).', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — A monarch butterfly’s chrysalis is one of the most beautiful things in nature. Bright emerald green and flecked with gold, it is an exquisite jewel that contains within it an even more exquisite promise. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1326, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/03/opinion/03renklWeb/03renklWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Last Butterfly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butterflies and Moths', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-03T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Renkl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Renkl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5faa7db2-ec90-5c65-bd9c-ce02a3f992f8', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5faa7db2-ec90-5c65-bd9c-ce02a3f992f8'}, {'abstract': 'Forty years ago, the Comme des Garçons designer began creating subversive, gender-bending clothes for men at a time when no one else was. She still is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/03/t-magazine/rei-kawakubo-comme-des-garcons-menswear.html', 'snippet': 'Forty years ago, the Comme des Garçons designer began creating subversive, gender-bending clothes for men at a time when no one else was. She still is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EARLY ONE MORNING, in the beginning of April, just as the cherry blossoms came into full bloom in Tokyo, 100-odd people gathered at the entrance to an anonymous office building in Aoyama, an elegant and sedate neighborhood known for its designer stores and expensive French restaurants. Unlike the building’s facade (squat, brick, unremarkable) or its interior (empty, beige, somehow even more unremarkable), the people making their way inside were, like the pink petals feathering the city’s sidewalks, impossible to ignore. Some looked like religious fundamentalists or figures from 16th-century portraits, others like extras from “La Strada.” They nodded and held doors open for one another, but remained quiet and unsmiling.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '111', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/27/t-magazine/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/08/27/t-magazine/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/27/t-magazine/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/27/t-magazine/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1100, 'width': 1649, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/27/t-magazine/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy/27tmag-rei-slide-5JST-copy-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'widt