 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c6371c0-201b-5f0d-8ab6-e37a0a13e823', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c6371c0-201b-5f0d-8ab6-e37a0a13e823'}, {'abstract': 'If you know beforehand how justices will vote based on which president appointed them, then what’s the point of having a court that, in theory, operates above politics?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/03/opinion/supreme-court-kavanaugh-partisan.html', 'snippet': 'If you know beforehand how justices will vote based on which president appointed them, then what’s the point of having a court that, in theory, operates above politics?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Justice Antonin Scalia died in early 2016, liberals and conservatives alike agreed that the next justice could transform American life for a generation or more. Donald Trump warned that if Hillary Clinton got to make the appointment “our country is going to be Venezuela.” Mrs. Clinton responded that a Trump justice “would threaten the future of our planet.” The partisan forecasts are even more ominous as Senate hearings begin next week on Mr. Trump’s nomination of Brett Kavanaugh to succeed Anthony M. Kennedy on the Supreme Court.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/31/opinion/31kaplan/merlin_106705054_1e7843f9-6793-4a8c-98c9-1317f42ef9d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/08/31/opinion/31kaplan/merlin_106705054_1e7843f9-6793-4a8c-98c9-1317f42ef9d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/08/31/opinion/31kaplan/merlin_106705054_1e7843f9-6793-4a8c-98c9-1317f42ef9d1-jumbo.jpg', 'he