'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haight, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-04T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Games', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06ec9a5b-6661-5e4f-af11-a54dcd55b305', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06ec9a5b-6661-5e4f-af11-a54dcd55b305'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time, we get a glimpse of Saul, and with it, a possible foretaste of Jimmy’s undoing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/03/arts/television/better-call-saul-season-4-episode-5-recap.html', 'snippet': 'For the first time, we get a glimpse of Saul, and with it, a possible foretaste of Jimmy’s undoing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, look who’s here. At long last, Saul Goodman, in the flesh. Four years and five episodes into the show that is named for him, television’s sketchiest plaintiff’s attorney has finally turned up. Sadly, he lingers only for a matter of minutes, in the midst of a frenzied effort to shred documents, collect cash and skedaddle out of town.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/arts/04saul-recap/04saul-recap-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Better Call Saul’ Season 4, Episode 5 Recap: Tell ’em Jimmy Sent You', 'kicker': 'Better Call Saul', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Better Call Saul (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-04T02:15:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6de2936c-c063-5390-ac8c-7a5a57831191', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6de2936c-c063-5390-ac8c-7a5a57831191'}, {'abstract': 'We imagine it as a concern of the powerful, hunched over the chessboard of the world. But strategic thinking is critical to broad movements, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/03/magazine/strategy-may-be-more-useful-to-pawns-than-to-kings.html', 'snippet': 'We imagine it as a concern of the powerful, hunched over the chessboard of the world. But strategic thinking is critical to broad movements, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1960s, the veteran radical Saul Alinsky looked at American society and did not like what he saw. With the antiwar and civil rights movements at critical junctures, many young activists seemed to be forgetting how to build and sustain power, turning instead toward showy street protest and random acts of violence. So Alinsky published, in 1971, his landmark book, “Rules for Radicals” — a guide to “tactics, maneuvers, strategy and principles of action in the making of revolutions.” Using terms that might have been plucked from a military manual, he sought to wrest the high art of strategy away from the “Haves” of the world and give it to the “Have-Nots.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/09/magazine/09mag-firstwords-image1/09mag-firstwords-image1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/09/magazine/09mag-firstwords-image1/09mag-firstwords-image1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/09/magazine/09mag-firstwords-image1/09mag-firstwords-image1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/09/magazine/09mag-firstwords-ima