ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Smarter Living', 'section_name': 'Smarter Living', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b720b117-9f1a-56c7-ac47-08e3b178ed42', 'word_count': 1877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b720b117-9f1a-56c7-ac47-08e3b178ed42'}, {'abstract': 'A new bill will help its intelligence agencies circumvent encryption. And what starts Down Under won’t necessarily stay there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/04/opinion/australia-encryption-surveillance-bill.html', 'snippet': 'A new bill will help its intelligence agencies circumvent encryption. And what starts Down Under won’t necessarily stay there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — A state’s capacity to spy on its citizens has grown exponentially in recent years as new technology has meant more aspects of our lives can be observed, recorded and analyzed than ever before. At the same time, much to the frustration of intelligence agencies around the world, so has the ability to keep digital information secret, thanks to encryption. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/opinion/04oshea/04oshea-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/04/opinion/04oshea/04oshea-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/opinion/04oshea/04oshea-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/04/opinion/04oshea/04oshea-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank':