', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd9ec456-9a6f-5fbe-b36e-723bd205e451', 'word_count': 1392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd9ec456-9a6f-5fbe-b36e-723bd205e451'}, {'abstract': 'Trump has torn through some establishment restraints on his conduct, but his weakness is still the major story of his presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/05/opinion/trump-bob-woodard-new-book.html', 'snippet': 'Trump has torn through some establishment restraints on his conduct, but his weakness is still the major story of his presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid the Resistance-y funeral rites of John McCain, the president’s latest Twitter rants against his attorney general and the wild White House stories being circulated by Bob Woodward’s latest book, it’s a good time to revisit a familiar and crucial subject. To what extent is Donald Trump an extraordinarily dangerous president whose authoritarian style is constantly enabled by his advisers and his party? Or, alternatively, to what extent is he an extraordinarily weak president, constrained by his appointees and his notional allies at almost every turn?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05douthatWeb/05douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05douthatWeb/05douthatWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05douthatWeb/05douthatWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '