e, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Melnick/05Melnick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Melnick/05Melnick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Melnick/05Melnick-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blame Emergency Rooms for the Out-of-Control Cost of Health Care', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Limit Hospitals’ Pricing Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-05T19:15:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Melnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Melnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/33c8b9b9-9a20-58f9-814b-544b38cd9df5', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33c8b9b9-9a20-58f9-814b-544b38cd9df5'}, {'abstract': 'The death of the storied alternative weekly isn’t just the end of a newspaper. It’s the end of New York as the lodestar for new and uncomfortable ideas. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/05/opinion/end-village-voice.html', 'snippet': 'The death of the storied alternative weekly isn’t just the end of a newspaper. It’s the end of New York as the lodestar for new and uncomfortable ideas. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The death last week of The Village Voice, the storied alt-weekly, was in some ways to be expected. When its latest owner, Peter D. Barbey, who bought it in 2015 to restore it to its early glory, stopped print publication almost a year ago, it seemed that it would be only a matter of time before its online presence ceased as well. The Voice didn’t appear to have a strong sense of identity anymore, in part because the New York that it covered — downtown, the underground, bohemia and its ephemera — didn’t exist anymore, neither in a physical sense nor as a state of mind. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/opinion/05Romano1/05Romano1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Rites for the Village Voice, a Bohemian Who Stayed On Too Long', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Village Voice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-05T19:22:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tricia Romano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b24530b-1c6e-5a81-a049-b3ba0c1b2fec', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b24530b-1c6e-5a81-a049-b3ba0c1b2fec'}, {'abstract': 'I work for the president but like-minded colleagues and I have vowed to thwart parts of his agenda and his worst inclinations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/05/opinion/trump-white-house-anonymous-resistance.html', 'snippet': 'I work for the president but like-minded colleagues and I have vowed to thwart parts of his agenda and his worst inclinations.', 'lead_paragraph': '[On Oct. 28, 2020, Miles Taylor, who left his position as chief of staff in the Department of Homeland Security, made public his authorship of this article. While The Times has a strict policy of protecting its sources, in this case he personally waived our agreement to keep his identity confidential. We can confirm that he is the author.]', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0,