'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e83442f8-fb75-5fbe-8a7e-af84058acd16', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e83442f8-fb75-5fbe-8a7e-af84058acd16'}, {'abstract': 'The instinct to use law enforcement tactics to make parents feel less anxious about mass shootings is misguided.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/06/opinion/school-shootings-security-violence.html', 'snippet': 'The instinct to use law enforcement tactics to make parents feel less anxious about mass shootings is misguided.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the school year begins, administrators across the country will rededicate themselves to protecting the safety of their students, including taking steps to prevent the kinds of mass shootings that have become all too common. Often, these steps involve what’s known as target hardening — things like adding automatic lockdown systems and hiring additional police officers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/06/opinion/06Keels/06Keels-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The False Comfort of Securing Schools', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'False Comfort in School Security', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings and Armed Attacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-06T15:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micere Keels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micere', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keels', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/31b457d8-e934-5cda-9114-df5ccf431e10', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31b457d8-e934-5cda-9114-df5ccf431e10'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Hal” looks at one of the most admired American directors of the 1970s, whose films include “Shampoo” and “Being There.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/06/movies/hal-review-documentary.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Hal” looks at one of the most admired American directors of the 1970s, whose films include “Shampoo” and “Being There.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the director Hal Ashby is mentioned now, he’s often squeezed into a cohort called New Hollywood, or as I think of it AltmanBogdanovichCoppolaFriedkinScorsese. This lineup changes depending on who’s telling the story and to what revisionist or orthodox end. Members, almost always men, are added, deleted or downgraded in the footnotes. Warren Beatty plays a role, as does the upstart Steven Spielberg. Ashby — who died at 59 in 1988 and whose films include “Being There” — remains another constant, a mainstay in a group that stormed the old studios in the late 1960s, changing Hollywood forever. (That’s one take, anyway.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/07/arts/07hal-1/merlin_143158131_cd3a0242-a0f6-4ed1-810c-33c52c8691cb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/07/arts/07hal-1/merlin_143158131_cd3a0242-a0f6-4ed1-810c-33c52c8691cb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/07/arts/07hal-1/merlin_143158131_cd3a0242-a0f6-4ed1-810c-33c52c8691cb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/07/arts/07hal-1/merlin_143158131_cd3a0242-a0f6-4ed1-810c-33c52c8691cb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 's