s', 'value': 'Helmreich, William B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-06T16:16:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cca61532-8cca-5579-a732-e8f99bb28cc0', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cca61532-8cca-5579-a732-e8f99bb28cc0'}, {'abstract': 'Mansoura has been serving Turkish delight, baklava and other kosher treats in Gravesend, Brooklyn, since 1961.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/06/nyregion/mansoura-bakery.html', 'snippet': 'Mansoura has been serving Turkish delight, baklava and other kosher treats in Gravesend, Brooklyn, since 1961.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost 20 years ago, Stanley Mieses, a writer and editor, first accompanied the guitarist Yosi Piamenta to Mansoura Pastries to buy knafeh, an Arabic dessert usually made with shredded phyllo dough. Per Mr. Piamenta’s instruction that day, he ordered the darker, crunchier knafeh pieces from the edges of the pan. It was some of the best knafeh he’d ever tasted in New York.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/09/nyregion/09JOINT1/merlin_143284332_dcbdb59e-1e04-457a-b416-6542df7150d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/09/nyregion/09JOINT1/merlin_143284332_dcbdb59e-1e04-457a-b416-6542df7150d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/09/nyregion/09JOINT1/merlin_143284332_dcb