ank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-06T19:36:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Obits', 'section_name': 'Obituaries', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ralph Blumenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ralph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blumenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/d612bdd0-c6b1-542d-a9ab-f25c13b7c887', 'word_count': 2214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d612bdd0-c6b1-542d-a9ab-f25c13b7c887'}, {'abstract': 'Is it O.K. to deny a neighbor a lift to the polling place based on her political views?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/06/style/voting-polls-political-views.html', 'snippet': 'Is it O.K. to deny a neighbor a lift to the polling place based on her political views?', 'lead_paragraph': 'My wife, kids and I live in the house where I grew up, across the street from a woman who was a dear old friend of my parents. Judging by her lawn signs, she has become much more politically extreme than when I was young. The coming midterm elections are important to my family: My wife works for a group that helps poor people get health care, and my son identifies as gay. In the last election, our (widowed) neighbor asked for a ride to the polls. I agreed. But now I wonder: How rude would it be to refuse her a ride and make her find another way to vote against my family’s interests?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'x