-a14a-6a9aca0d99e4', 'word_count': 1276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fc9ee80-d87e-5f67-a14a-6a9aca0d99e4'}, {'abstract': 'Ana Khouri’s enchantment with antique vessels began as a child in São Paulo; now she has more than 60 of them. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/07/t-magazine/ana-khouri-apothecary-bottles.html', 'snippet': 'Ana Khouri’s enchantment with antique vessels began as a child in São Paulo; now she has more than 60 of them. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Manhattan-based Jewelry designer Ana Khouri’s enchantment with antique apothecary vessels began as a child in São Paulo, where she was born and raised. Her grandfather, a doctor, had an antique pharmacy cabinet at his farmhouse upstate, and Khouri, 37, remembers being “mesmerized by the beautiful bottles.” She has since begun her own collection of these medicinal relics, which lined pharmacy shelves from medieval times until as recently as the 1950s. The designer’s 60-odd jars and bottles — some made from thick, amber glass, others from Delftware and Limoges porcelain — once contained remedies derived from flora and fauna. In addition to creating her sculptural, boundary-pushing fine jewelry — delicate rose-gold rings that span two fingers, diamond half-moon hoops that float above the earlobe — Khouri has become something of an amateur herbalist. “I am fascinated by how botanicals can cure or cause harm, depending on how they are used,” she said. Her new work will be exhibited at the Phillips auction house in New York City from Sept. 14 to Sept. 16. ', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '134', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-khouri-slide-00ZY/07tmag-khouri-slide-00ZY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/07/t-magazine/07tmag-khouri-slide-00ZY/07tmag-khouri-slide-0