pg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Your Generation Experience More Anxiety Than Other Generations?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2018-09-10T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c90ad8f-76b7-5d6a-9acc-327a513d6bf9', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c90ad8f-76b7-5d6a-9acc-327a513d6bf9'}, {'abstract': 'Procedures live on even after they’ve been proved ineffective. It can lead to harms and wasted resources. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/10/upshot/its-hard-for-doctors-to-unlearn-things-thats-costly-for-all-of-us.html', 'snippet': 'Procedures live on even after they’ve been proved ineffective. It can lead to harms and wasted resources. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'We know it can be hard to persuade physicians to do some things that have proven benefits, such as monitor blood pressure or keep patients on anticoagulants. But it might be even harder to get them to stop doing things.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/upshot/11up-healthoveruse1/11up-healthoveruse1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/11/upshot/11up-healthoveruse1/11up-healthoveruse1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capt