he fish was caught. The company’s salmon jerky, in four varieties, is made from whole strips of sustainably harvested wild Alaskan keta salmon. The jerky, smoked in British Columbia, is chewy, briny and satisfying. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1095, 'width': 1954, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/dining/12burner4/12burner4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Salmon Jerky for Snacking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Nibble: This Salmon Jerky Includes a Lesson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salmon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snack Foods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fishpeople Seafood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-10T15:18:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0008a240-1932-5212-b243-3a67fb7a2c35', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0008a240-1932-5212-b243-3a67fb7a2c35'}, {'abstract': 'Migrating birds can become disoriented as they fly at night. But the 9/11 Tribute in Light has come up with a solution. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/10/opinion/9-11-tribute-in-light-birds.html', 'snippet': 'Migrating birds can become disoriented as they fly at night. But the 9/11 Tribute in Light has come up with a solution. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every Sept. 11, beams of light rise more than four miles into the night sky from the National 9/11 Memorial & Museum in Lower Manhattan to honor the nearly 3,000 lives lost in America’s most devastating terrorist attacks. The glowing columns elegantly trace the outlines of the twin towers. But they also beckon thousands of migrating birds into the city’s skyscraper canyons, where they can become disoriented and crash headlong into buildings. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/10/opinion/10farnsworthWeb/merlin_143410668_30a8ae87-1c08-44c6-be54-40643e3d90cc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/10/opinion/10farnsworthWeb/merlin_143410668_30a8ae