 by Chelsea Hodson.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 759}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 810, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1619, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-newsentences1/16mag-newsentences1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Sentences: From Chelsea Hodson’s ‘Tonight I’m Someone Else’', 'kicker': 'New Sentences', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Chelsea Hodson’s ‘Tonight I’m Someone Else’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Tonight I'm Someone Else: Essays (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hodson, Chelsea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a0cf873-730b-5f8e-93b6-4a40ccaa255d', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a0cf873-730b-5f8e-93b6-4a40ccaa255d'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/books/review/new-noteworthy-barry-gewen.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIS LAND By Dan Barry. (Black Dog & Leventhal, $29.99.) For a decade, from 2007 to 2017, Barry’s column for The Times explored everyday life and everyday people in America — from a hairdresser in Vicco, Ky., to the owner of a small oil company in Dixfield, Me. This book collects nearly 100 of his columns, providing a panoramic view of the country as it passed from Bush to Obama to Trump. THE FABULOUS BOUVIER SISTERS By Sam Kashner and Nancy Schoenberger. (Harper, $28.99.) A book-length exploration of the complicated sister love between Jackie Kennedy Onassis and Lee Radziwill (née Bouvier) — their shared appreciation for fashion and art, as well as the intense jealousy that characterized their relationship. THE ART OF LOGIC IN AN ILLOGICAL WORLD By Eugenia Cheng. (Basic, $27.) Cheng is a mathematician who believes we need to appreciate the value of alogic — emotion, that is — if we want to understand a world filled with irrational behavior. Yet she also thinks smartly applied logic might help address some of our problems. ACCESSORY TO WAR By Neil deGrasse Tyson and Avis Lang. (Norton, $30.) The celebrity astrophysicist and a research associate at the Hayden Planetarium examine the ways military branches have used the science of astrophysics to bolster their power. It’s an alliance between science and war-making that has been, Tyson and Lang write, “curiously complicit.” THE PARTING GIFT By Evan Fallenberg. (Other Press, $19.95.) An erotic, mysterious novel set in Israel that takes the form of a letter. The unnamed narrator describes a consuming love affair that threatens his own well-being and that of the man with whom he has fallen in love.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70c6b3c6-06a3-54b4-b1bc-d83568f48e7b', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70c6b3c6-06a3-54b4-b1bc-d83568f48e7b'}, {'abstract': 'Fictional and autobiographical tales that span the vast range of the educator’s spirit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/books/review/teacher-education-school-novels-memoirs.html', 'snippet': 'Fictional and autobiographical tales that span the vast range of the educator’s spirit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Match Book,', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1287, 'width': 1058, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/03/16/books/review/16matchbook-image/16matchbook-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fall Back Into the School Year With Books by, for and About Teachers', 'kicker': 'Match Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Lamy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lamy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/976ac8bc-3915-5e65-9ed2-c79df38ea5ab', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/976ac8bc-3915-5e65-9ed2-c79df38ea5ab'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t turn the conversation to yourself. Offer support. Find out if it’s O.K. to tell others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/magazine/how-to-respond-when-someone-comes-out-to-you.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t turn the conversation to yourself. Offer support. Find out if it’s O.K. to tell others.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Remember that it’s not about you,” says Telaina Eriksen, a creative-writing professor at Michigan State University in East Lansing, who wrote a book about her daughter’s coming out as a lesbian. No matter what kind of relationship you have with the person, don’t immediately turn the conversation to yourself by saying something like “I knew it all along!” or “How could you do this to me?” If you are in a position of authority — a parent, teacher or coach — be extra careful; what you say will be imbued with that power differential. “Whatever you do,” Eriksen says, “don’t say, ‘Are you sure?’\u2009”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Mag-Tip-1/16Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Mag-Tip-1/16Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'ra