th the new show ‘Maniac,’ he continues bringing an auteur’s sensibility — and a fanatical attention to detail — to the small screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/magazine/true-detective-director-cary-fukunaga-netflix-maniac.html', 'snippet': 'With the new show ‘Maniac,’ he continues bringing an auteur’s sensibility — and a fanatical attention to detail — to the small screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of 2009, the director Cary Joji Fukunaga was hunting for his second feature film. His first, “Sin Nombre,” had been an almost preposterously ambitious undertaking for a first-time filmmaker, tracking the intertwining stories of an immigrant and an erstwhile member of MS-13 as they made the dangerous journey to the United States border atop fast-moving train cars, all written by Fukunaga in slang-heavy Spanish. Over the course of two years, he made multiple trips to Mexico, riding the real-life version of these trains and visiting prisons in Chiapas to make connections with gang members who could help ensure that the film had a documentarylike accuracy. “I need to believe what I’m watching,” he says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Fukunaga1/16Fukunaga1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘True Detective’ Director Cary Fukunaga Is Bringing His Obsessions to Netflix', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fukunaga, Cary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Maniac (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Jonah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stone, Emma', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sin nombre (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'True Detective (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Willa Paskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Willa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00f54970-25f4-5810-a219-95b7122083ef', 'word_count': 3469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00f54970-25f4-5810-a219-95b7122083ef'}, {'abstract': 'Followers of the bizarre “QAnon” theory imagine they can follow preposterous internet hints to some great truth. But that’s rarely where a trail of crumbs takes you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/magazine/a-trail-of-bread-crumbs-leading-conspiracy-theorists-into-the-wilderness.html', 'snippet': 'Followers of the bizarre “QAnon” theory imagine they can follow preposterous internet hints to some great truth. But that’s rarely where a trail of crumbs takes you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Go hungry for too long, and a lot of strange things will start to look like food. The smallest morsels become precious, especially if you believe they form some kind of trail with a meal at the end. This is true not only of physical sustenance; it is true of knowledge as well. You always find scattered crumbs — inscrutable analogies, esoteric equations, unverified allegations from anonymous sources — gathered around those questions about which we know the least.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-FirstWords-image1/16mag-FirstWords-image1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trail of ‘Bread Crumbs,’ Leading Conspiracy Theorists Into the Wilderness', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inquiring Minds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Prosecutors (Independent Counsel)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mattathias Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mattathias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8088cd34-2ae8-579d-8d82-f1e9c324dc6f', 'word_count': 1535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8088cd34-2ae8-579d-8d82-f1e9c324dc6f'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. unemployment is down and jobs are going unfilled. But for people without much education, the real question is: Do those jobs pay enough to live on?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/magazine/americans-jobs-poverty-homeless.html', 'snippet': 'U.S. unemployment is down and jobs are going unfilled. But for people without much education, the real question is: Do those jobs pay enough to live on?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vanessa Solivan and her three children fled their last place in June 2015, after a young man was shot and killed around the corner. They found a floor to sleep on in Vanessa’s parents’ home on North Clinton Avenue in East Trenton. It wasn’t a safer neighborhood, but it was a known one. Vanessa took only what she could cram into her station wagon, a 2004 Chrysler Pacifica, letting the bed bugs have the rest.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-articleLarge.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-jumbo.png', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-promo-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-promo-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16Poverty2-promo/16Poverty2-promo-thumbLarge-v3.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans Want to Believe Jobs Are the Solution to Poverty. They’re Not.', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Work Doesn’t Work Anymore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Desmond', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Desmond', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f24855d6-94ad-58bc-9d4b-9887d77bb337', 'word_count': 5407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f24855d6-94ad-58bc-9d4b-9887d77bb337'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on preserving racially prejudiced — or otherwise bigoted — historical artifacts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/magazine/what-should-i-do-with-old-racist-memorabilia.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on preserving racially prejudiced — or otherwise bigoted — historical artifacts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While renovating our home 30 years ago, we found an album of old postcards lodged in a rafter. Many were from the turn of the century, addressed to members of a family that settled in our community. A brief search at the time did not turn up any descendants.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-ethicist-image1/16mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-ethicist-image1/16mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-ethicist-image1/16mag-ethicist-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-ethicist-image1/16mag-ethicist-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/magazine/16mag-ethicist-image1/16mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 't