, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at the Morgan Library & Museum shows why fantastical creatures from the Middle Ages continue to terrify and inspire awe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/arts/design/medieval-monsters-review-morgan-library.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Morgan Library & Museum shows why fantastical creatures from the Middle Ages continue to terrify and inspire awe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the first manuscripts you see upon entering the show “Medieval Monsters: Terrors, Aliens, Wonders” at the Morgan Library & Museum is a 1595 edition of the Flemish cartographer Abraham Ortelius’s “Theatrum Orbis Terrarum” (“Theater of the World”). The book is open to a map of Iceland featuring rows of mountains, a lava-spewing volcano and an array of monsters, including one that looks like a horse crossed with a mermaid and a prehistoric sea creature. Ortelius’s “Theatrum” is regarded as the first modern atlas, yet, as the exhibition text points out, it still adheres to a medieval view of the world. Fanciful images representing the strangeness and danger of the unknown accompany geographic information.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14morganreview3/merlin_143108532_500866cf-94d9-472f-b4cc-813c7cdc16c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14morganreview3/merlin_143108532_500866cf-94d9-472f-b4cc-813c7cdc16c0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14morganreview3/merlin_143108532_500866cf-94d9-472f-b4cc-813c7cdc16c0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 