k': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Valentino Fashion Group SpA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yves Saint Laurent', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liana Finck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14ea7a7a-317c-52fb-92de-323aeaaf527c', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14ea7a7a-317c-52fb-92de-323aeaaf527c'}, {'abstract': 'As video of the runaway ball went viral again, its creator, Kurt Perschke, discussed how it’s safely traveled the world. But he gets why people love the clip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/arts/design/red-ball-twitter.html', 'snippet': 'As video of the runaway ball went viral again, its creator, Kurt Perschke, discussed how it’s safely traveled the world. But he gets why people love the clip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If art is meant to bring joy, this work certainly did: a giant red 250-pound ball that broke free of its handlers, bounced down a street in Toledo, Ohio, over a couple of cars and into internet fame for a hot minute in 2015.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/merlin_128830541_cb101b6f-af30-4606-bfa2-7f561b473a85-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/merlin_128830541_cb101b6f-af30-4606-bfa2-7f561b473a85-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/merlin_128830541_cb101b6f-af30-4606-bfa2-7f561b473a85-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/merlin_128830541_cb101b6f-af30-4606-bfa2-7f561b473a85-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/12redball4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/12redball4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12redball4/12redball4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Red Ball That Bounced Through Toledo (in 2015) Is on the Move Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Runaway Was Caught, Then Went Back Out on the Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perschke, Kurt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'RedBall (Exhibit)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toledo (Ohio)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chapel Hill (NC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:40:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sopan Deb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sopan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Deb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09a6da55-dec5-5204-b801-a4490460cb62', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09a6da55-dec5-5204-b801-a4490460cb62'}, {'abstract': 'Speech on Twitter and Facebook should not be treated like a collective good that should be subject to political control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/opinion/the-slippery-slope-of-regulating-social-media.html', 'snippet': 'Speech on Twitter and Facebook should not be treated like a collective good that should be subject to political control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The promise of the internet, and social media in particular, was that it would not only allow anyone the opportunity to speak, but would also make it possible for anyone to precisely tailor what he reads, sees and hears online. News and information would no longer be mediated by newspaper editors, television producers and other gatekeepers. Instead, social media would allow direct access to individual voices in a feed custom-built by the user. It was a new frontier for both unregulated free expression and individual control.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1374, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11suderman/11suderman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Slippery Slope of Regulating Social Media ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:45:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Suderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c384550e-9901-54fb-8e6b-eaab1c94b4f2', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c384550e-9901-54fb-8e6b-eaab1c94b4f2'}, {'abstract': 'In the elegant, readable and sobering “These Truths,” Lepore starts with Columbus’s arrival and wends her way through the next five centuries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/books/review-these-truths-history-united-states-jill-lepore.html', 'snippet': 'In the elegant, readable and sobering “These Truths,” Lepore starts with Columbus’s arrival and wends her way through the next five centuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We know how all of this is going to end: with Donald J. Trump.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 896}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1867, 'width': 1250, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/12/arts/12booklepore1/12booklepore1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As the News Cycle Lurches, Jill Lepore Offers a Long, Steady View of American History', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reckoning for the Republic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'These Truths: A History of the United States (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepore, Jill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/41eec13e-8c43-5623-93ec-4b5434efb8af', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41eec13e-8c43-5623-93ec-4b5434efb8af'}, {'abstract': 'Squint and you see the complex stories that attracted the filmmakers to the life of Richard Wershe Jr., a Detroit dealer arrested at 17 in 1987.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/movies/review-white-boy-rick.html', 'snippet': 'Squint and you see the complex stories that attracted the filmmakers to the life of Richard Wershe Jr., a Detroit dealer arrested at 17 in 1987.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A fiction built on nonfiction, “White Boy Rick” traces the grim, grubby rise and predictable fall of Richard Wershe Jr. (the newcomer Richie Merritt), a Detroit drug dealer who was arrested in 1987 at 17. Rick Jr. earns his catchy nickname soon after he starts running with powerful black street gangsters who inexplicably welcome him into their fold, as if he were one of them. (The White Boy name might have been a media fantasy about the character’s putative exoticism, at least that’s what the real Mr. Wershe suggested to The Guardian in 2015. “They did it to glorify or glamorize,” he said, as if he were “an anomaly.”)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/merlin_143453505_c6b94651-248b-41b0-88ea-bf1dacf1b032-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/merlin_143453505_c6b94651-248b-41b0-88ea-bf1dacf1b032-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/merlin_143453505_c6b94651-248b-41b0-88ea-bf1dacf1b032-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/merlin_143453505_c6b94651-248b-41b0-88ea-bf1dacf1b032-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/14whiteboyrick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/14whiteboyrick-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14whiteboyrick/14whiteboyrick-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Drugs, Guns and Poses in ‘White Boy Rick’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Only Teenage Wasteland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Demange, Yann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConaughey, Matthew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'White Boy Rick (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merritt, Richie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:54:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5bafad8-3b2b-5a6d-933d-c8fe7f484a0c', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5bafad8-3b2b-5a6d-933d-c8fe7f484a0c'}, {'abstract': 'Chloë Sevigny plays the famous mom-and-pop slayer as a woman desperate for liberation in 19th-century Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/movies/lizzie-review-chloe-sevigny-kristen-stewart-borden.html', 'snippet': 'Chloë Sevigny plays the famous mom-and-pop slayer as a woman desperate for liberation in 19th-century Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something wildly freeing about the savage killings in “Lizzie,” a distinctly feminist take on the notorious Lizzie Borden, history’s most famous, if unproven, mom-and-pop slayer. This sense of liberation derives from murders enacted with the methodical exhilaration of a jailbreak — a cathartic response to years of oppression by her miserly father, Andrew (Jamey Sheridan), and loathed stepmother, Abby (Fiona Shaw). And when Lizzie strips naked before hacking her two tormentors to slivers, her nudity isn’t simply practical: It’s the repudiation of a 19th-century wardrobe that controlled women’s movements as thoroughly as men did.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1437, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/14/arts/14lizzie/14lizzie-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Lizzie,’ an Oppressed Daughter Driven to Murder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Oppressed Daughter Is Driven to Murder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lizzie (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sevigny, Chloe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Kristen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheridan, Jamey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macneill, Craig William (1975- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-11T18:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6eafaefb-8a0d-537e-b1dc-ad6a7cf94ca8', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6eafaefb-8a0d-537e-b1dc-ad6a7cf94ca8'}, {'abstract': 'Doctors and hospitals love to talk about the cancer patients they’ve saved, and reporters love to write about them. But deaths still vastly outnumber the rare successes. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/11/opinion/cancer-genetic-testing-precision-medicine.html', 'snippet': 'Doctors and hospitals love to talk about the cancer patients they’ve saved, and reporters love to write about them. But deaths still vastly outnumber the rare successes. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Facing incurable breast cancer at age 55, MaryAnne DiCanto put her faith in “precision medicine” — in which doctors try to match patients with drugs that target the genetic mutations in their tumors. She underwent repeated biopsies to identify therapies that might help. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/11/opinion/11szabo/11szabo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Are We Being Misled About Precision Medicine?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'va