_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jose A. Del Real and Matt Stevens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jose', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Del', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e511f4d-c4e7-50a0-be13-3a2a16056f39', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e511f4d-c4e7-50a0-be13-3a2a16056f39'}, {'abstract': 'The challenge stirred scorn and ridicule, until the presidential spokesman welcomed the suggestion and Parliament moved to legalize dueling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/14/world/europe/navalny-duel-russia-zolotov.html', 'snippet': 'The challenge stirred scorn and ridicule, until the presidential spokesman welcomed the suggestion and Parliament moved to legalize dueling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Over the years, the Kremlin has reached deep into its bag of tricks to silence Aleksei A. Navalny, the anti-corruption campaigner and opposition leader. He has been repeatedly jailed, harassed by tax auditors and security agents, attacked with green dye and castigated by the state news media as a traitorous lackey of the West.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/15/world/15Navalny-1-print/merlin_143587215_210cd7b3-f95f-4b13-937b-2dc2aa0775bc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/15/world/15Navalny-1-print/merlin_143587215_210cd7b3-f95f-4b13-937b-2dc2aa0775bc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/15/world/15Navalny-1-print/merlin_143587215_210cd7b3-f95f-4b13-937b-2dc2aa0775bc-jumbo.j