iginal': 'By Ronda Kaysen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaysen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8904de75-adb7-5d1d-b616-4079aa97fc87', 'word_count': 1379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8904de75-adb7-5d1d-b616-4079aa97fc87'}, {'abstract': 'The 76-year-old performer will debut a new program that celebrates 1950s gospel, black spirituals and her own songbook in October.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/14/arts/music/amina-claudine-myers-gospel.html', 'snippet': 'The 76-year-old performer will debut a new program that celebrates 1950s gospel, black spirituals and her own songbook in October.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amina Claudine Myers was sitting in her apartment in Hell’s Kitchen, surrounded by paintings and photographs as she recalled a rehearsal there almost 40 years ago. She’d been playing the same baby grand piano that sits in the living room today, and Cecil McBee had brought his upright bass. She recalled that sounds came spontaneously to her lips. “I do remember thinking: You don’t need words to express,” she said. “Just your voice, the sound, can relay a message.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '104', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/arts/16AMINA1/16AMINA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/arts/16AMINA1/16AMINA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/arts/16AMINA1/16AMINA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N