material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1fc72b5-c005-58ab-b38f-90868d3d09be', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1fc72b5-c005-58ab-b38f-90868d3d09be'}, {'abstract': 'Political and technological disruption have fed off each other since the nation’s founding. Now they are dangerously out of whack. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/14/sunday-review/politics-disruption-media-technology.html', 'snippet': 'Political and technological disruption have fed off each other since the nation’s founding. Now they are dangerously out of whack. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every government is a machine, and every machine has its tinkerers — and its jams. From the start, machines have driven American democracy and, just as often, crippled it. The printing press, the telegraph, the radio, the television, the mainframe, cable TV, the internet: Each had wild-eyed boosters who promised that a machine could hold the republic together, or make it more efficient, or repair the damage caused by the last machine. Each time, this assertion would be both right and terribly wrong. But lately, it’s mainly wrong, chiefly because the rules that prevail on the internet were devised by people who fundamentally don’t believe in government.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/opinion/16lepore/16lepore-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/16/opinion/16lepore/16lepore-articleLarge-v2.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/16/opinion/16lepore/16lepore-jumbo-v2.gif', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N