': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-opinionvideo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-17T14:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taige Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taige', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b8e37749-38a9-5e0f-a60c-07fa4e4e2ec6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b8e37749-38a9-5e0f-a60c-07fa4e4e2ec6'}, {'abstract': 'Working largely outside the gallery system, a group of illustrators is reviving the discipline and redefining how queer bodies are represented in art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/17/t-magazine/gay-queer-figure-art.html', 'snippet': 'Working largely outside the gallery system, a group of illustrators is reviving the discipline and redefining how queer bodies are represented in art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAST FALL, IN a tiny apartment in downtown New York, a 30-year-old gay physique model named Matthew Williams stood naked against a white backdrop in front of the gay artist John MacConnell. “It’s liberating to be able to be comfortable in your body,” Williams said, barely moving his lips as he concentrated on holding still. The 33-year-old MacConnell — boyish, equally fit — wore black jeans and a white T-shirt as he sketched on a letter-size sheet of paper with his blue ballpoint pen. “It’s important for me to capture likeness and not just a body,” he said.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '158', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gayfigure-slide-3A29-copy/05tmag-gayfigure-slide-3A29-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name