, 'lastname': 'Libbey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edb94788-97eb-5f5e-bc15-bf2ba81968ce', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edb94788-97eb-5f5e-bc15-bf2ba81968ce'}, {'abstract': "Once among the city's greatest cultural institutions, George Balanchine’s creation lost its way under his successor, Peter Martins.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/17/opinion/new-york-city-ballet-decline-fall.html', 'snippet': "Once among the city's greatest cultural institutions, George Balanchine’s creation lost its way under his successor, Peter Martins.", 'lead_paragraph': 'George Balanchine and Lincoln Kirstein spent their lifetime creating what was possibly the most beautiful, fragile and deeply historied arts institution in American history — New York City Ballet. Their achievement is all the more miraculous for its having ever existed at all. Mr. Kirstein often said he was amazed each night as the curtain rose, so tenuous was the whole enterprise.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/17/opinion/17Bentley-2/merlin_143893203_23d73404-213c-422e-a7ec-9be1e8fc631e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/17/opinion/17Bentley-2/merlin_143893203_23d73404-213c-422e-a7ec-9be1e8fc631e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/17/opinion/17Bentley-2/merlin_143893203_23d73404-213c-422e-a7ec-9be1e8fc631e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty