rdings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-19T17:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Video', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': "By Amanda Hess and Shane O'Neill", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Shane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Neill", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae8e1708-5e2c-5e57-91dd-cad925557706', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae8e1708-5e2c-5e57-91dd-cad925557706'}, {'abstract': 'The city can fight congestion and raise revenue through parking regulations. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/19/opinion/primus_nyc-traffic-congestion-parking.html', 'snippet': 'The city can fight congestion and raise revenue through parking regulations. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traffic is strangling New York’s economic vitality and quality of life, while the city’s transit system creaks because of chronic underinvestment. One solution, congestion pricing, would undoubtedly reduce traffic and generate desperately needed revenue for transit. But this has proved politically difficult in New York, both when  attempted in 2007 and again this year. Congestion pricing asks people to pay to drive on roads they used to drive on without charge, so the politics won’t get easier anytime soon. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/opinion/19primus/merlin_142165248_323c9947-0a40-4a62-bfd0-81d71abf5185-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/19/opinion/19primus/merlin_142165248_323c9947-0a40-4a62-bfd0-81d71abf5185-ar