preme Court (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Christine Blasey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kavanaugh, Brett M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-19T20:48:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd602034-3f57-5268-8360-4845466d8fe5', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd602034-3f57-5268-8360-4845466d8fe5'}, {'abstract': 'In London, A Sai Ta makes vibrantly colorful, innovative women’s wear that explores his mixed heritage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/19/t-magazine/fashion/asai-ta-designer-london-fashion-week.html', 'snippet': 'In London, A Sai Ta makes vibrantly colorful, innovative women’s wear that explores his mixed heritage.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s organized chaos in here,” says the British-Vietnamese designer A Sai Ta, standing in the middle of his tiny north London studio. Among the stacks of overstuffed plastic boxes exploding with garments — skimpy transparent neon tops, a pair of American-flag-patterned pants, scarlet sweaters trimmed with fraying fringe — some eight provisional assistants are busy helping Ta put the finishing touches on his spring 2019 collection. Ta, 30, runs his studio alone, and so his friends have stepped in to help ensure everything gets finished: One volunteer hems tie-dyed fluorescent orange ribbon belts, another carefully glues plumes of sugar-pink and lime-green ostrich feathers to a pair of minimalist ’90s-era strappy sandals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/19/t-magazine/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V/19tmag-asai-slide-8N4V-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Designer Who References Asian Iconography and His Older Sisters’ Clubbing Phase', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asai (Fashion Label)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fashion East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'London Fashion Week', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-19T20:59:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Fashion & Beauty', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grace Cook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grace', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68493d7f-86ac-54b0-82c0-23243349989e', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68493d7f-86ac-54b0-82c0-23243349989e'}, {'abstract': 'Orthodox Jewish women and men live in tightly defined, and separate, spheres. The photographer Sharon Pulwer was given a rare invitation to enter the private world of Brooklyn’s most Orthodox Jewish women. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/19/nyregion/a-glimpse-inside-the-hidden-world-of-hasidic-women.html', 'snippet': 'Orthodox Jewish women and men live in tightly defined, and separate, spheres. The photographer Sharon Pulwer was given a rare invitation to enter the private world of Brooklyn’s most Orthodox Jewish women. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sharon Pulwer was lost in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, shortly after moving here from Israel to study photography, when she came across the black hats and modest clothes of religious Jews in New York City. A secular Jew, she was momentarily taken aback. “I was very surprised that there was this very vivid part of Jewish life here that I was not aware about.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/nyregion/23hasidicwomen01-promo/merlin_143695035_c7f59d7c-8b79-47cf-824f-8281b2e90458-articleLarge.jpg'