nk': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fallon, Jimmy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Christine Blasey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Kevin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Anita', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kavanaugh, Brett M', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Full Frontal With Samantha Bee (TV Program)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Late Night with Seth Meyers (TV Program)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Late Show with Stephen Colbert (TV Program)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Tonight Show (TV Program)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-20T08:54:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Giovanni Russonello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Giovanni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russonello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3edb85a-b54a-5860-a59e-e5fcfdcafb17', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3edb85a-b54a-5860-a59e-e5fcfdcafb17'}, {'abstract': 'With the 2018 prize postponed by scandal, The Times’s staff book critics discuss the award’s history and influence — and whom they would give it to this year if they could.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/20/books/review/nobel-prize-literature-critics-discussion.html', 'snippet': 'With the 2018 prize postponed by scandal, The Times’s staff book critics discuss the award’s history and influence — and whom they would give it to this year if they could.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right about this time of year is when the literary world would normally be praising, bemoaning or just scratching its collective head over the latest winner of the Nobel Prize in Literature. Alas, this year’s prize has been “postponed” (two will be awarded in 2019) because of a sex abuse scandal in the august halls of the Swedish Academy, which hands out the award. That scandal is being covered elsewhere in this newspaper. But to help fill the void of conversation around the prize itself, I recently spoke with The New York Times’s staff book critics — Dwight Garner, Parul Sehgal and Jennifer Szalai — about what the prize has meant (or not meant) to their own reading habits; their opinions of past winners (and past snubs); and whom they would bestow the honor upon this year if they could. — John Williams', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/books/30NobelTalk1/30NobelTalk1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 444, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/books/30NobelTalk1/30NobelTalk1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 444}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/books/30NobelTalk1/30NobelTalk1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 757, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/books/30NobelTalk1/30NobelTalk1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1515, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/books/30NobelTalk1/30NobelTalk1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnai