': 'By Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8f8f1b3-febd-5c94-8d90-376e7daa9931', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8f8f1b3-febd-5c94-8d90-376e7daa9931'}, {'abstract': 'Foodies, cyclists and the aesthetically inclined will find much to like in this desert city, home to a new bike-share system, adobe architecture and restaurants that reflect a mix of cultural influences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/20/travel/what-to-do-in-tucson.html', 'snippet': 'Foodies, cyclists and the aesthetically inclined will find much to like in this desert city, home to a new bike-share system, adobe architecture and restaurants that reflect a mix of cultural influences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Austin is to Texas, Tucson is to Arizona. In this outspoken university town, artists, intellectuals and athletes share their passions for good food and outdoor fun. In Tucson’s case, its location in the southern Sonoran Desert divides two sections of scenic Saguaro National Park where cactuses reside in multi-limbed groves. Two years ago, Unesco cited Tucson as the nation’s first City of Gastronomy, highlighting its mix of Native American, colonial Spanish and border Mexican influences. That recognition seems only to have lit the fuse on new and adventurous breweries and distilleries as well as restaurants. With challenging urban hikes, other, more remote, trails nearby, and a new bike share system, Tucson makes for a calorically balanced weekend.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Hours-Tucson1/merlin_143947515_d3236d91-2d13-4c7e-bf67-1eea4c017ac2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Tucson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tucson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-20T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63985a3c-8da5-56c9-993a-6c68eaac1003', 'word_count': 1420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63985a3c-8da5-56c9-993a-6c68eaac1003'}, {'abstract': 'The actor and author of the new book “Whiskey in a Teacup” wishes she had five days in a cabin just to read, with “no emails, no text messages and no obligations or deadlines.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/20/books/review/by-the-book-reese-witherspoon.html', 'snippet': 'The actor and author of the new book “Whiskey in a Teacup” wishes she had five days in a cabin just to read, with “no emails, no text messages and no obligations or deadlines.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actor and author of the new book “Whiskey in a Teacup” wishes she had five days in a cabin just to read, with “no emails, no text messages and no obligations or deadlines.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/merlin_143547768_10e8b2e4-8443-4f49-bcd4-d4cec1e7ce5f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/merlin_143547768_10e8b2e4-8443-4f49-bcd4-d4cec1e7ce5f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 623}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/merlin_143547768_10e8b2e4-8443-4f49-bcd4-d4cec1e7ce5f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 987, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/merlin_143547768_10e8b2e4-8443-4f49-bcd4-d4cec1e7ce5f-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1973, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/23ByTheBook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/23ByTheBook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23ByTheBook/23ByTheBook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By the Book: Reese Witherspoon', 'kicker': 'By the Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reese Witherspoon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witherspoon, Reese', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-20T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/084b2502-beea-5d9c-b4be-2089883d9da3', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/084b2502-beea-5d9c-b4be-2089883d9da3'}, {'abstract': 'All romance novels have happy endings. How they get there, though, is a lot more interesting when the protagonists must grapple with personal shortcomings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/20/books/review/tessa-dare-governess-game.html', 'snippet': 'All romance novels have happy endings. How they get there, though, is a lot more interesting when the protagonists must grapple with personal shortcomings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every romance novel is a kind of fantasy, whether it features an impossibly wealthy duke or a cute guy who knows how to talk about his feelings. It can be escapist or aspirational, extravagantly hyperbolic or easily plausible, but it’s still idealized. Every romance has a happy ending, after all. That’s a narrative constraint and a defining characteristic of the genre; it’s also a puzzle for an author to solve. Make your main characters too good or too perfect, and obstacles to their happy ending seem far-fetched. Make them too flawed, and maybe they don’t deserve a happy ending. Here are five new romances that grapple with their protagonists’ shortcomings and virtues — their worthiness of a happy ending — or at least leave their readers doing so after the final page. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23Green-ROMANCE/merlin_143560848_fa907c27-a41d-4621-b8c3-7db4082cbe00-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 621, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23Green-ROMANCE/merlin_143560848_fa907c27-a41d-4621-b8c3-7db4082cbe00-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 621}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23Green-ROMANCE/merlin_143560848_fa907c27-a41d-4621-b8c3-7db4082cbe00-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 989, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/23/books/review/23Green-ROMANCE/merlin_14356