': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Duke I Tempted (Book)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peckham, Scarlett (Author)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-20T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaime Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jaime', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43df1afd-aee8-5545-93dd-706e904a1aad', 'word_count': 1564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43df1afd-aee8-5545-93dd-706e904a1aad'}, {'abstract': 'The latest entree to join the Army’s roster of M.R.E. field rations is a Sicilian-style slice that stays fresh for years and took decades to develop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/20/us/army-pizza-mre-field-rations.html', 'snippet': 'The latest entree to join the Army’s roster of M.R.E. field rations is a Sicilian-style slice that stays fresh for years and took decades to develop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NATICK, Mass — The American military calls its combat field rations M.R.E.’s, for Meals, Ready to Eat, since they require no cooking. But the troops long ago decided that those initials stood for Meals Refused by Everyone. The stuff may have been filling, but it sure wasn’t appetizing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/17/us/00mrepizza-01/merlin_143722983_c0289595-0533-4785-9e37-0e16f1d471d4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/17/us/00mrepizza-01/merlin_143722983_c0289595-0533-4785-9e37-0e16f1d471d4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarge