at and crime suggests the need for more research on shootings in American cities. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The national murder rate reached a modern low in 2014, capping a quarter-century decline. Then it rose across the United States in 2015 and 2016.  Why gun violence increased in those two years remains somewhat of a mystery, and no single factor is likely to explain it, but there is one potential contributor that is rarely talked about: the weather. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-articleLarge.png', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-jumbo.png', 'height': 511, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-superJumbo.png', 'height': 821, 'width': 1646, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/21/upshot/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850/22up-heatcrime1-1537473012850-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thu