r a dam at the site breached and allowed coal ash to enter the nearby Cape Fear River. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/21/climate/florences-floodwaters-breach-defenses-at-power-plant-prompting-shutdown.html', 'snippet': 'Duke Energy shut down a power plant in Wilmington, N.C., after a dam at the site breached and allowed coal ash to enter the nearby Cape Fear River. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAVASSA, N.C. — With floodwaters continuing to rise in the wake of Hurricane Florence, state officials and environmentalists are closely monitoring the breach of a dam that has flooded a hazardous stockpile of coal ash, some of which has spilled into the Cape Fear River.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Florence’s Floodwaters Breach Defenses at Duke Energy Plant, Sending Toxic Coal Ash Into River', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Toxic Coal Ash Spills Into Cape Fear River', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Florence (2018)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke Energy Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cape Fear River (North Carolina)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-21T17:05:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Climate', 'section_name': 'Climate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Thrush and Kendra Pierre-Louis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thrush', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kendra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pierre-Louis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28800a8b-c34a-5260-8ba0-6c1fdcfdbcbc', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28800a8b-c34a-5260-8ba0-6c1fdcfdbcbc'}, {'abstract': 'Gerald Incandela’s massive studio in Torrington, Conn., has become the cornerstone of a more ambitious project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/21/t-magazine/gerald-incandela-photographer-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Gerald Incandela’s massive studio in Torrington, Conn., has become the cornerstone of a more ambitious project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT TWILIGHT ON a rainy Saturday in late fall, Torrington, Conn., feels like any rusted-out postindustrial Northeastern mill town: barely alive. Between abandoned brick factories, faded clapboard houses with tar-paper-covered roofs creak in the wind. Under the eaves of the Sons of Italy social club, patrons smoke beside a sign for Wednesday bingo. Although nestled in Litchfield County, the preternaturally preserved Revolutionary War-era enclave where many wealthy New Yorkers keep second homes, Torrington remains an uncharted territory to most of those residents.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/21/t-magazine/21tmag-incandela-slide-88A1/21tmag-incandela-slide-88A1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/21/t-magazine/21tmag-incandela-slide-88A1/21tmag-incandela-slide-88A1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 921}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '