re asylum seekers. On their 18th birthdays they became criminals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fall of 2017, Wilmer Ramirez had reason to be hopeful. After he trekked from Guatemala and spent several months in a youth migrant shelter in Arizona, his application for “special immigrant juvenile status,” a designation that would make him eligible for lawful permanent residency, was pending. What’s more, a family in Pennsylvania had agreed to sponsor him, meaning they would submit themselves to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for approval and take him in once they were approved. The only thing between him and freedom, then, was a little more paperwork.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/22/opinion/22migrantkids/merlin_144108258_3f0726ee-8027-44a1-bf91-3eedb1e37dd5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/22/opinion/22migrantkids/merlin_144108258_3f0726ee-8027-44a1-bf91-3eedb1e37dd5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 712}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/22/opinion/22migrantkids/merlin_144108258_3f0726ee-8027-44a1-bf91-3eedb1e37dd5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 863, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/22/opinion/22migrantkids/merlin_144108258_3f0726ee-8027-44a1-bf91-3eedb1e37dd5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1185, 'width': 998, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/22/opinion/22migrantkids/22migrantkids-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType