': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37e0db02-afc9-5b97-a60c-23b544883459', 'word_count': 4726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37e0db02-afc9-5b97-a60c-23b544883459'}, {'abstract': 'A book on Victorian marriage doubles as a case for the transformative power of gossip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/25/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-phyllis-roses-parallel-lives.html', 'snippet': 'A book on Victorian marriage doubles as a case for the transformative power of gossip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer I asked almost everyone I met to tell me the story of his or her life. (We were in the right season for gossip — confidences rise and fall with the humidity index.) I was not so concerned with honesty. “True story” is already something of an oxymoron. Instead I wanted the character arcs people wrote for themselves, tales that ended with the moment they sat down on a chair in front of me. The good gossips among them knew to deflect — “Tell me the story of your life” — but no matter who went first, we always started, or claimed to start, at the beginning.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30Mag-LOR/30Mag-LOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30Mag-LOR/30Mag-LOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 432}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30Mag-LOR/30Mag-LOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30Mag-LOR/30Mag-LOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1474, 'width': 2048, '