ption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/upshot/26up-kavapoll1/merlin_144254463_e299e130-98b9-48d2-9033-606f8579cc21-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1560, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/upshot/26up-kavapoll1/26up-kavapoll1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/26/upshot/26up-kavapoll1/26up-kavapoll1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/upshot/26up-kavapoll1/26up-kavapoll1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Voters in Battleground Districts Are Divided on Kavanaugh', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kavanaugh, Brett M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2018)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-25T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/577a85f0-3f85-51c3-9031-2be54b9831e2', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/577a85f0-3f85-51c3-9031-2be54b9831e2'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/25/books/review/new-noteworthy-joe-coscarelli.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN YOU GROW UP TO VOTE By Eleanor Roosevelt with Michelle Markel. Illustrated by Grace Lin. (Roaring Brook, $19.99.) Roosevelt wrote this children’s primer on democracy in 1932, the same year her husband was elected president. She wanted young people to know the many arms of government and, as she put it, that “these people work for YOU.” CLIMATE JUSTICE By Mary Robinson. (Bloomsbury, $26.) The former president of Ireland, inspired by the birth of her first grandchild, has turned her attention to the environment and what can be done to fight climate change. THE INCURABLE ROMANTIC By Frank Tallis. (Basic, $27.) People driven mad by love is the stuff of operas. But Tallis, a clinical psychologist, takes a serious look at the neuroscience of the phenomenon, focusing on sensational case studies — like the man who kept 3,000 prostitutes in his employ, spending all his money in the hopes that they would fall in love with him. BECOMING LINCOLN By William W. Freehling. (University of Virginia, $29.95.) The Civil War historian takes a look at Lincoln’s upbringing, and the rough road that led him to the presidency. CALL THEM BY THEIR TRUE NAMES By Rebecca Solnit. (Haymarket, paper, $15.95.) This collection of essays by Solnit, dubbed the “voice of the resistance,” explores a range of topics, including the role of emotions in our political life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/books/review/3