d the U.N.', 'kicker': 'New York Today', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-25T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Wolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/59c43bcc-1147-5074-b1f8-4a02d6dd1ab3', 'word_count': 1206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59c43bcc-1147-5074-b1f8-4a02d6dd1ab3'}, {'abstract': 'Revelations about Brett Kavanaugh and Rod Rosenstein got the week started on a tumultuous note. Where are we headed now?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/25/opinion/kavanaugh-rosenstein-blasey-ford.html', 'snippet': 'Revelations about Brett Kavanaugh and Rod Rosenstein got the week started on a tumultuous note. Where are we headed now?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bret Stephens: Good morning, Gail. I know we’ll have plenty to say about Blasey v. Kavanaugh today, but, first, Rod Rosenstein! The Times had a bombshell story last week saying the deputy attorney general felt so badly used by President Trump last year after the firing of James Comey that he considered wearing a wire to record the president’s ranting. Rosenstein denied it categorically and the Republican establishment urged Trump not to fire him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/24/opinion/24conversation1web/merlin_143268189_341b1f17-8089-48e2-8278-33a76f9f15a5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/24/opinion/24conversation1web/merlin_143268189_