e', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Company (Play)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-26T14:46:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5789ab8-4f49-59a1-bfdc-bd4a41f72db1', 'word_count': 1960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5789ab8-4f49-59a1-bfdc-bd4a41f72db1'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Trump said he favored a two-state solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. His administration has been working on a peace plan for nearly two years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/26/world/middleeast/trump-israel-palestinians.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Trump said he favored a two-state solution to the Israeli-Palestinian conflict. His administration has been working on a peace plan for nearly two years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Trump said Wednesday that he hoped to present his long-anticipated peace plan for Israel and the Palestinians within four months, and said he favored a two-state solution to the decades of conflict between the two sides.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump Vows to Release Mideast Peace Plan Within 4 Months', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peace Plan Is Near, Says U.S. President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-26T14:59:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8df6b637-3cee-5482-b452-a319b03061ef', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8df6b637-3cee-5482-b452-a319b03061ef'}, {'abstract': 'Too many Americans would deny Muslims the religious liberty they insist upon for Christians. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/26/opinion/islamophobia-muslim-religion-politics.html', 'snippet': 'Too many Americans would deny Muslims the religious liberty they insist upon for Christians. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Religious liberty has become a particularly politicized topic in recent years, and recent months were no different. In a long-awaited June decision, the Supreme Court decided in favor of a Christian baker who refused to make a custom wedding cake for a gay couple. In July, Attorney General Jeff Sessions introduced a “religious liberty task force” that critics saw as a mere cover for anti-gay discrimination. And Judge Brett Kavanaugh’s record has been scoured for evidence of what his appointment to the Supreme Court would mean for future decisions in which Christian beliefs clash with law and policy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/26/opinion/26Uddin/26Uddin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Latest Attack on Islam: It’s Not a Religion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Religious Double Standard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Religion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-26T15:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Asma T. Uddin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Asma', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Uddin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/909471a0-6af8-5360-8e2a-a33f5ad4f42a', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/909471a0-6af8-5360-8e2a-a33f5ad4f42a'}, {'abstract': 'The new director of the Bronx Museum of the Arts designed her apartment around a colorful work by a lifelong mentor, the printmaker Robert Blackburn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/26/arts/design/show-us-your-wall-deborah-cullen.html', 'snippet': 'The new director of the Bronx Museum of the Arts designed her apartment around a colorful work by a lifelong mentor, the printmaker Robert Blackburn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Next to the table where Deborah Cullen eats breakfast, there’s an orange, red and blue abstract print by Robert Blackburn, the lithographer who founded the Printmaking Workshop in Chelsea. A gift titled “Blue Things 1963-70,” it has an inscription beginning, “To Debbie.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28showwall-promo/merlin_143694750_57ec8ad0-844c-4186-8095-05f22a2d6582-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28showwall-promo/merlin_143694750_57ec8ad0-844c-4186-8095-05f22a2d6582-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28showwall-promo/merlin_143694750_57ec8ad0-844c-4186-8095-05f22a2d6582-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 