nization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a14357a0-ff28-5025-bea6-2920f3fe2264', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a14357a0-ff28-5025-bea6-2920f3fe2264'}, {'abstract': 'In Jeremy Saulnier’s murky follow-up to “Green Room,” Jeffrey Wright goes on an adventure with Riley Keough, Alexander Skarsgard and a pack of wolves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/26/movies/hold-the-dark-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Jeremy Saulnier’s murky follow-up to “Green Room,” Jeffrey Wright goes on an adventure with Riley Keough, Alexander Skarsgard and a pack of wolves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Something weird this way comes, or so it seems in the shadowy, overly murky “Hold the Dark.” There are the wolves prowling in that gloom that may be snatching children from Keelut, an isolated, sparsely populated Alaskan hamlet. Despite their alleged dietary habits, the wolves are the area’s most appealing inhabitants and not just because they don’t come across as dangerous or damaged as the human residents. Unlike all the scowling men and women, these howling beauties — who look as well-groomed as they appear well fed and trained — are also fun to watch.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28holdthedark/merlin_143986797_d7b7eb92-fdba-4d74-93bb-00275c62b969-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28holdthedark/merlin_143986797_d7b7eb92-fdba-4d74-93bb-00275c62b969-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/28/arts/28holdthedark/merlin_143986797_d7b7eb92-fdba-4d74-93bb-00275c62b969-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumb