s of the birds could have weighed more than 1,700 pounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/26/science/largest-elephant-bird.html', 'snippet': 'A study seeks to restore the elephant bird’s heavyweight title, finding one member of a previously unidentified genus of the birds could have weighed more than 1,700 pounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'History has not been kind to the elephant bird of Madagascar. Standing nearly 10 feet tall and weighing up to 1,000 pounds — or so researchers believed — this flightless cousin of the ostrich went extinct in the 17th century, thanks in part to humans stealing their massive eggs, either to feed their own families or to repurpose them as giant rum flasks. Or both.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/02/science/26TB-BIRD1/merlin_144305559_321e475b-728d-4f9b-baeb-928b1a953d10-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 776, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/02/science/26TB-BIRD1/merlin_144305559_321e475b-728d-4f9b-baeb-928b1a953d10-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 776}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/02/science/26TB-BIRD1/merlin_144305559_321e475b-728d-4f9b-baeb-928b1a953d10-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/02/science/26TB-BIRD1/merlin_144305559_321e475b-728d-4f9b-baeb-928b1a953d10-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1650, 'width': 1275, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/02/science/26TB-BIRD1/26TB-BIRD1-thumbStandard.jp