e title and number of lines: Fourteen. It’s both a nod to the sonnet and to the age of her female protagonist, a biracial “nerd-jock” struggling to fit in as she navigates the bewildering flux of adolescence. This poem deftly volleys between acceptance on the soccer field, where “brown is/an international color,” and anger provoked by racial and sexual slurs encountered in the everyday adult world. Selected by Rita Dove', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 929, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 929, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/magazine/30poem1/30poem1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poem: Fourteen', 'kicker': 'Poem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fourteen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-27T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Swearingen-Steadwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swearingen-Steadwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32f91444-1a99-5cb8-a3c6-3bb1e7db17ca', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32f91444-1a99-5cb8-a3c6-3bb1e7db17ca'}, {'abstract': 'Do cheese and crackers count? What about if you add pickles?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/27/magazine/judge-john-hodgman-on-what-constitutes-a-meal.html', 'snippet': 'Do cheese and crackers count? What about if you add pickles?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad writes: My wife, Kristena, says her favorite meal is meat, cheese and pickles on a cracker. I believe that is a snack, not a meal. When asked if she would like a special birthday dinner consisting of only that, she said yes! She even claims that this snack becomes a sandwich if another cracker is placed on top. Please bring justice before my wife’s delusions get worse.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/mag-hodgman-image/mag-hodgman-image-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/08/magazine/mag-hodgman-image/mag-hodgman-image-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima