 'value': 'Full Frontal With Samantha Bee (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-28T09:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy A. Ruhling', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Ruhling', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/576d2347-ca26-5f16-b5fc-691330899f38', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/576d2347-ca26-5f16-b5fc-691330899f38'}, {'abstract': 'Thrift has worked its way from Airbnb mania to spinoff hotel brands and independent properties.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/28/travel/budget-design-hotels.html', 'snippet': 'Thrift has worked its way from Airbnb mania to spinoff hotel brands and independent properties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you haven’t visited a college campus, secondhand store or seen “Crazy Rich Asians,” in which one of the central characters possesses a Jamba Juice freebie card, you may not have heard: Thrift is cool. In hospitality, that spirit has worked its way from Airbnb mania to spinoff hotel brands and independent properties that promise the travel equivalent of fast fashion.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/travel/30Getaway-2/30Getaway-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Hip to Be Cheap as Hotels Democratize Design', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Taste of ‘Cheap Chic’ for a Night’s Stay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budget Travel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-09-28T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f76f4e05-165f-51c1-b255-3c1d7ad6b678', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f76f4e05-165f-51c1-b255-3c1d7ad6b678'}, {'abstract': 'SD Hotels is capitalizing on the hype around two distinct travel trends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/28/business/sd-hotels-luxury.html', 'snippet': 'SD Hotels is capitalizing on the hype around two distinct travel trends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the breakfast buffet of Borgo Egnazia, a high-end resort in Fasano on Italy’s eastern coast, there’s a carafe of a substance unlikely to be found at any similarly luxurious hotel elsewhere in the world: siero di latte e cacao, or milk serum and cocoa. It looks like hot chocolate but tastes like seaweed, pungent enough that it might trigger the gag reflex of a consumer more accustomed to starting the morning with something from Starbucks. A centuries-old breakfast staple in this part of the world, the drink is essentially a protein shake made with the whey that remains after milk has been curdled and strained.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/business/30sdhotels1/merlin_142095168_17a93251-6a42-43af-8304-456c7a4064c4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/business/30sdhotels1/merlin_142095168_17a93251-6a42-43af-8304-456c7a4064c4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/business/30sdhotels1/merlin_142095168_17a93251-6a42-43af-8304-456c7a4064c4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/business/30sdhotels1/merlin_142095168_17a93251-6a42-43af-8304-456c7a4064c4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type