tion_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/23f6e321-59da-5707-b467-4f31bc08c830', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23f6e321-59da-5707-b467-4f31bc08c830'}, {'abstract': 'A proposed citizenship question on the 2020 census reveals the dependency between older white voters and America’s growing young minority population.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/30/opinion/trump-cant-win-the-war-on-demography.html', 'snippet': 'A proposed citizenship question on the 2020 census reveals the dependency between older white voters and America’s growing young minority population.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the early days of his campaign, from his proposal to build a wall along the Mexican border to his discredited committee on voter fraud, President Trump has declared war on America’s changing demography. His administration has followed through on that strategy with a proposal to add a question to the 2020 census asking about citizenship. If the question remains on the form, millions of households, particularly Hispanic and Asian-American, could skip the census, leading to an overrepresentation of white Americans during this once-a-decade count.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/opinion/30Frey/30Frey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/30/opinion/30Frey/30Frey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/30/opinion/30Frey/30Frey-j